{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 44, :comments ["<b>And despite even this.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): it means, even though I am destined to bring upon them this calamity, I will not reject them in exile, but I will keep the covenant for them." "<b>And by way of the Midrash</b> — it says זאת concerning the Torah, concerning which it is written (Deuteronomy 4:44) וזאת התורה, and it comes to hint that the Torah will not depart from them even when they are in exile in the land of their enemies, in the manner of what He promised in the verse (Deuteronomy 31:21) כי לא תשכח מפי זרעו. And from this the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, expounded in Torat Kohanim: 'And what was left to Israel that they were not rejected and not abhorred? Were not all the good gifts given to Israel taken from them? And were it not for the Torah that was left to them, the nations would have left them nothing' — they intended by this what I have said." "<b>I did not reject them.</b> In the days of Vespasian. <b>And I did not abhor them.</b> In the days of the Greeks. <b>To destroy them, to break My covenant with them.</b> In the days of Haman. <b>For I am YHWH their Elohim.</b> In the days of Gog and Magog, when no creature can prevail against them." "<b>And you will find</b> in this parashah, from 'ואם לא תשמעו לי' until 'געלה נפשם,' three hundred and ninety words, and in none of them is there a mention of the Name, and it comes to hint to the three hundred and ninety years that the tribes sinned in the land, on account of what they said (II Samuel 20:1) אין לנו חלק בדוד ולא נחלה לנו בבן ישי וגו׳. And you will find in Chronicles (I Chronicles 16) 'ושבועתו לישחק' with a shin, and the reading (kri) with a tzadi — the Holy One, blessed be He, swore to him that if his sons do not sin more than three hundred and ninety years He will not exile them from the land, and this is what Ezekiel said (Ezekiel 4) 'ואני נתתי לך את שני עונם למספר ימים שלש מאות ותשעים יום, יום לשנה יום לשנה יום לשנה נתתיו לך.'" "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'ואף גם זאת' includes Kneset Yisrael, which is called זאת, as it is said (Psalms 118:23) מאת ה' היתה זאת, (Genesis 49:28) וזאת אשר דבר להם אביהם. And the intention is that in every place where Israel were exiled, the Shekhinah was with them, and this is what the verse says: לא מאסתים ולא געלתים לכלתם is written — for the sake of their bride (כלתם), I will not break My covenant with them. And this is what Asaph mentioned (Psalms 80:15) הבט משמים וראה ופקוד גפן זאת — הבט משמים וראה as in (I Kings 8:32) ואתה תשמע השמים, and ופקוד — visit the vine of זאת. And he explained in the midst of his words that the vine of זאת is renowned for the wickedness called 'חזיר יער,' and I have already mentioned this in the verse (Genesis 36:31) ואלה המלכים אשר מלכו בארץ אדום, and there you will see it." "<b>And know</b> that by way of the Kabbalah there is a great difference between the expression אף and the expression גם, even though both are expressions of increase; for the expression אף alludes to the attribute of justice and the expression גם alludes to the attribute of mercy, from the language of גמילות חסדים (bestowing kindnesses), as in (I Samuel 15:22?) ״וגם שמע בקולי״ — and hearing is in mercy. And this is ״ואף גם זאת״ — for the word זאת is inclusive of justice and mercy, and this is clear."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {3 "The abbreviation ש\"ץ is resolved as 390 (shin=300 + tzadi=90) based on the gematria argument that follows; retained in Hebrew in the body per divine-name and number rules."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:29:10.122296874Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}